{
  "dom_id": "step-q184176",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Prove that ${n}^{2}-2$ is odd provided that $n$ is odd.\n\nSince $n$ is odd, it can be expressed as $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{2}-2$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-2 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}-2={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, ${n}^{2}-2$ is {{field-7}}.",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
